Mint Pulse for Cinnamon

Start Mint Pulse from the application menu or run /usr/bin/mint-pulse.
Enable the XApp Status Applet on a horizontal top or bottom Cinnamon panel.
The package does not enable autostart for every user. To start it at login,
add /usr/bin/mint-pulse to Cinnamon Startup Applications.

Colors can be selected with --theme auto, dark, light or high-contrast.
Use --thresholds 90,75,50,85 to set CPU, RAM, swap and filesystem warning levels.
Use --path / to monitor the root filesystem instead of the home filesystem.
Run /usr/bin/mint-pulse --help for all options.

If a manually installed version is already running, exit that process before
starting the packaged version. Review existing Startup Applications entries:
an absolute path in ~/.local/bin can continue to launch an older binary.
Use /usr/bin/mint-pulse for the packaged version. The package never edits home
directories or deletes an earlier manual installation.

The SSD label represents filesystem occupancy, not disk I/O or drive health.
Missing data and disabled swap appear as an em dash. Use a local filesystem.
Theme auto-detection uses the theme name; custom names may need an override.
